## 4. Apply to vertex-disjoint terminal pentagon walls

Take physical unit pentagons F_1,...,F_p whose source-vertex sets are pairwise
disjoint, as supplied in the applicable branch by canonical
C-PENTAGON-WALL-DISJOINT-BULK.

Let R be any nonempty set of vertices chosen from these walls. Put r=|R| and let

[
t=
#{	ext{chosen pentagon boundary edges with both endpoints in }R}.
]

Every selected wall vertex is incident with exactly two boundary edges of its
chosen pentagon. The chosen pentagon vertex sets are disjoint, so these wall
edges are distinct across the family. Double counting incidences shows that
exactly 2r-t selected pentagon boundary contacts are lost. Other old contacts
can only increase the debt. Therefore

[
oxed{c_{m lost}ge2r-t.}
	ag{4.1}
]

For r>=3, equations (3.4) and (4.1) give

[
2r-tle2r-4,
]

hence every replacement satisfying Section 3 must obey

[
oxed{tge4.}
	ag{4.2}
]

For r=1 or 2, the small planar caps give an immediate contradiction for every
possible choice from the pentagon walls.

If Y subset int(K) and the deleted set removes an old diameter pair, (3.5)
instead gives

[
oxed{tge5.}
	ag{4.3}
]

Thus internal exact contacts cannot make sparse guard deletion free. The
deletion set must already contain at least four original pentagon wall edges,
or at least five when the diameter-pair equality case is invoked.

## 5. Consequences

### 5.1 One guard per wall

Choose one vertex from each of any p>=1 pairwise vertex-disjoint terminal
pentagons. Then r=p and t=0. No same-cardinality packet strictly unit-clear
from every retained old source point can repair the deletion, even if the
inserted points may have arbitrary exact unit contacts among themselves.

For p>=3, the walls lose at least 2p contacts while the global replacement
packet has at most 2p-4 internal contacts.

### 5.2 Two nonadjacent vertices per wall

Choose two nonadjacent wall vertices from each selected pentagon. Again t=0,
now r=2p and c_lost>=4p. A two-point replacement has at most one internal
contact when p=1; for p>=2,

[
c_{m new}le4p-4<4ple c_{m lost}.
]

So this deletion pattern is impossible under the Section 3 replacement
contract, even allowing contacts between replacement points assigned to
different walls.

## 6. Scope check: adjacency is a genuine escape from this proof

Deleting two adjacent vertices from each of p disjoint pentagons gives

[
r=2p,qquad t=p,qquad c_{m lost}ge3p.
]

The global planar cap is 4p-4 for p>=2. Contact counting alone rules this out
only for p<=3. At p=4 the lower bound equals the planar cap; if a diameter pair
is lost and the replacement is interior, the secondary normalization still
rules out equality. For larger p, this proof alone gives no contradiction.

Therefore the live exact-contact mechanism is sharply localized: a collective
repair must concentrate deleted wall vertices along old wall adjacencies, or
create new unit contacts to retained old vertices.

## 7. Target C consequence

Audit.59 leaves exact-contact discrete repair alive after strict-safe area
averaging fails. In the terminal-pentagon branch, this result removes two
natural global repair patterns: one deleted guard per wall, and two
nonadjacent deleted vertices per wall.

A surviving contact-breaking/refill theorem must instead do at least one of:

- delete a pattern containing at least four old pentagon wall adjacencies
  (at least five when the diameter-pair equality case applies);
- make exact unit contacts from inserted points to retained old source points;
- use a finite source motion rather than same-cardinality replacement;
- or obtain the strict sub-D contradiction by another Target C route.

This is a proof-only narrowing of the audit.59 repair space. It does not close
C-II, assert existence of a replacement packet, or treat new-old exact contacts.
No computation or verifier output is a proof premise.
